#X text 45 88 [zbiplot] plots biquad coefficients as poles and zeros on the Z-plane. Poles are drawn as crosses and zeros as circles \, inside the unit circle \, with the real axis running left to right., f 66;
#X text 45 140 Click and drag a pole or a zero to move it \, and hold shift for fine tuning. A point above the real axis is half of a complex conjugate pair \, so its mirror image below the axis moves with it., f 46;
#X text 45 205 Dragging sends the coefficients out of the outlet. They only come out while the plot holds two poles and two zeros \, counting each conjugate pair as two., f 46;
#X text 45 258 Coefficients are in [biquad~] order \, the same as [else/bicoeff2] and [else/biplot] use., f 46;
#X obj 340 136 else/zbiplot;
#X obj 340 345 else/display;
#X obj 45 300 bng 17 250 50 0 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 #dcdcdc #000000 #000000;
#X obj 45 325 else/bicoeff2 eq 500 10 24;
#X obj 45 350 else/insert coeff;
#X text 70 296 load a filter into the plot \, then drag it around, f 32;
#N canvas 103 54 1438 875 example 0;
#X text 30 20 Coefficients go in and come out in [biquad~] order - fb1 fb2 ff1 ff2 ff3 - so [else/bicoeff2] feeds the plot directly and the outlet drives [biquad~] directly., f 64;
#X text 30 72 Drag the poles and zeros and you are filtering the noise by hand. Turn the volume down first: poles can be dragged outside the unit circle \, and that makes the filter blow up., f 64;
